<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Bellini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1430–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Bellini was a Venetian painter who, more than anyone, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> a leading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> art. His altarpieces, such as the San <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria">Zaccaria</a> Altarpiece, and his many Madonnas used oil paint, rich colour and natural light in new ways. He ran a busy workshop, became official painter to the republic, and taught or influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgione">Giorgione</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-bellini.jpg" alt="Giovanni Bellini" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Bellini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Bellini_Felt%C3%A9telezett%C3%96narck%C3%A9peKJ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giovanni Bellini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and raised in the household of the painter Jacopo Bellini, long considered his father. The art historian Daniel Wallace Maze claims instead that Jacopo was his much elder half-brother and that Gentile Bellini was his nephew rather than his brother. Gentile was more highly regarded than Giovanni during their lifetimes. His brother-in-law was the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-mantegna">Andrea Mantegna</a>, to whose work his early paintings are often linked, and those early works are all in the old tempera method. In 1470 he received his first commission, to work with Gentile and others in the Scuola di San Marco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After 1479 or 1480 much of his time went to his duties as conservator of the paintings in the great hall of the Doge's Palace, for which he received a fixed annual pension of eighty ducats. He repaired the work of predecessors and painted new subjects on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s part in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and the pope. None of these survived the fire of 1577, and much of his public work is lost.</p><p>His religious works survive in greater number. He gradually mastered the oil medium that Antonello da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> about 1473, using it for blended colour and atmospheric gradation of tone. The San Giobbe Altarpiece and the San Zaccaria Altarpiece are both of the Holy Conversation type, with the Madonna enthroned beneath a golden half dome recalling St Mark's. In the later work the light is soft and diffuse, and the altarpiece, dated 1505, is considered perhaps the most imposing of his altarpieces. Later altarpieces include those at San Francesco della Vigna in 1507 and San Giovanni Crisostomo in 1513.</p><p>In his last years he was besieged with more commissions than he could complete. Isabella Gonzaga of Mantua had difficulty obtaining a painting for which she had paid in part in advance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer">Albrecht Dürer</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1506, called him very old but still the best in painting. In 1514 he undertook The Feast of the Gods for Duke Alfonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bellini died in 1516 and was buried in the Basilica di San Giovanni e Paolo. He outlived the Vivarini rivals and his brother Gentile, who died in 1507. His pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgione">Giorgione</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, and also Vittore Belliniano, Rocco Marconi and Andrea Previtali. His Coronation of the Virgin is credited with introducing single-panel altarpieces, the pala, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and he joined oil painting's realism to Venetian and Byzantine traditions of colour and iconography. His most important contribution is thought to lie in his experiments with colour and atmosphere in oil.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Bellini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Venice was also one of the great artistic centres of Europe: its school of painting included <b>Giovanni Bellini</b>, Giorgione, Titian, Tintoretto and Paolo Veronese, Andrea Palladio built churches and villas that were copied for centuries, and composers from Claudio Monteverdi to Antonio Vivaldi worked in the city, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1485" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giovanni Bellini"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5734 6163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5734 6163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1485">What was here in 1485? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
